clean_summary:
Interesting episode. Initially, the CEO agent in project vend seemed to mimic company operations, but it was introduced because the LLM was too aligned, making the main agent overly helpful—offering too many discounts and not focusing enough on profit.

Andon Labs' cofounders @lukaspet and @axelbacklund explain why dollar-denominated evaluations reveal aspects that traditional benchmarks miss. They discuss how Claude reported a $2/day vending machine fee to the FBI, how long-horizon agents can spiral in unexpected ways, what occurs when agents lie or form price cartels, and how agents competing with each other can behave. They argue that the future of AI safety may rely on testing models in messy real-world environments rather than in clean benchmark sandboxes.

